```cs
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int Old_x = x;
    int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

    bool New_Ret = arrList.Contains(x);

    int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);
    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX);
    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    // @@@ If the return value is true, index of x before is less than or equal to after 
    Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret == true && (Old_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListIndexOfX));
}
```

In the code block above, the `Assert.AreEqual` method is used to check if the count, indexOf, and lastIndexOf properties of the ArrayList object remain unchanged. The `Assert.IsTrue` method is used to check if the return value of the `Contains` method is true and the index of x before is less than or equal to the index after the element is added to the ArrayList.--------------------I'll analyze the code and make some observations regarding the assert statements:

